Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:13.190 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  [Music]  Silent Hill it's a game series that has  captivated me since my childhood  childhood you say yes I among many  others were way too young when we were  first exposed to these games I think I  was around the age of 12 and back then I  didn't own a Playstation myself me and  my younger cousin were Nintendo boys but  occasionally the two of us would stay  over at another cousins house who lived  far away together the three of us would  play games on his PlayStation which  granted me the opportunity to experience  games that under normal circumstances I  would not be able to play we finished  Resident Evil one two and three which  were already pretty intense for a  twelve-year-old but then my cousin  pulled out a different game Silent Hill  and it scared us even more than Resident  Evil ever did made by Konami and  published in 1999 the game was  originally intended to be a direct  competitor to Resident Evil but due to  clashing ideas as well as inexperienced  among the development team the game was  shaped into something very different a  more deep and personal experience the  team that developed the game was dubbed  team silent and following the unexpected  success of the first game continued to  make multiple sequels after that I want  to refrain from all the drama  surrounding Konami so long story short  eventually team silent got disbanded and  Silent Hill IP was handed over to  Western developers who would continue  the series onward most Silent Hill fans  agree that the games developed by the  original team silent are the golden  years of Silent Hill and the franchise  has since then degraded in quality hence  for this analysis I'm only focusing on  the first three games from the team  silent era I'm going to exclude Silent  Hill a room  despite also being developed by team  silent it's so fundamentally different  from the others  this video would pretty much double in  size but I might cover it separately in  a future video  [Music]  to someone who is unfamiliar with the  series it's hard to explain exactly why  the Japanese titles are so much better  on the surface level aesthetically some  of the Western made games look rather  similar to their Japanese counterparts  however there was a shift in Soul  unlike Resident Evil which was more  in-your-face Silent Hill was all about  subtlety the smallest of sounds  symbolism and imagery that can be easily  missed if you're not paying attention  the Western developers made the game's a  little bit more action focused and the  monsters became less and less creative  and additionally it also lost a lot of  the symbolism that made the older game  so interesting every monster design  visual clue or music track was put there  for a reason I mentioned in my previous  video that darkness can provide an  additional challenge by disorienting the  player in that regard  welcome to Silent Hill  an area that under normal circumstance  would be pretty straightforward to  navigate suddenly becomes a  claustrophobic maze even in the  overworld you have a very limited field  of view in the original this was  initially implemented to hide some of  the technical shortcomings of the  PlayStation one and in particular the  limited draw distance the town of Silent  Hill which the series is named after is  a pretty big place even in the first  game the streets city blocks and squares  can stretch out quite far so to deal  with this technical limitation fog was  used to mask some of these shortcomings  what this ended up doing however was  create a very oppressive and  claustrophobic atmosphere even when  you're out in the open and actually  became the main staple of Silent Hill as  a far covered town for both the games  and the movie adaptations to come  indoors this is amplified even further  with a field of view of perhaps only a  few meters up until Silent Hill the room  that is where you didn't even have a  flashlight so now let's get into the  level design as there's plenty to go  over when discussing Silent Hill we have  to talk atmosphere first as this is by  far the greatest strength of the series  it's what captivated me in the first  place and made it stand out from any  other game I played previously I have  somewhat of a fascination for old and  abandoned cities and buildings and used  to be quite the urban explorer myself so  if that's your thing then Silent Hill  will definitely strike a chord with you  Silent Hill is a ghost town it's streets  and houses buildings long forgotten and  abandoned you can still find many traces  of former human activity scattered  around the streets and the buildings  newspapers shopping carts garbage bags  furniture and more giving off a very  post-apocalyptic vibe but most of all as  soon as you set foot onto the streets of  Silent Hill a feeling of dread hangs  over you like a dark cloud this isn't  just some regular abandoned town one of  the most prominent ways this is  communicated to the player is through  the use of music and ambience there  doesn't even have to be anything  significant happening visually the audio  says it all  when it comes to silent hill's  foreboding atmosphere the sound design  plays one of the most if not the most  important role the brilliant mind behind  these amazing tracks and ambient Tunes  is sound director Akira Yamaoka who was  present from the very first game and  even continued to compose for the  western made installments all the way up  to 2012 Silent Hill downpour one of the  last remnants of team silent yamaoka  creative experimentation stands out by  the use of very industrial percussive  and weirdly distorted sound design even  going as far as to use the sound of a  dentist's drill during one of the boss  fights the sound of a dentist's drill is  of course already not very pleasant so  this creates a very unnerving feeling  when it comes to the soundtrack itself  there's also a flipside pure silence  Silent Hill knows when to shut up the  sudden absence of music creates a great  contrast when all you hear is your own  footsteps  for some of the ambient noises in the  background the team created a wide  variety of randomized sounds that can  play at any time and at different  intervals keeping the experience unique  even after multiple playthroughs to this  day I still sometimes discover new  sounds I've never heard before combine  all of this with the pitch-black  hallways disturbing imagery and feeling  of isolation and you have the recipe for  a very effective horror experience since  we are primarily talking about dungeon  and level design as opposed to story and  development history behind the games I'm  going to make some comparisons to the  Legend of Zelda series because of its  nature as a fantasy series the temples  and dungeons in those games are for the  most part very quote-unquote videogame  II even if a place is called a temple  shrine or tower it's hard to imagine  that people actually lived here or  visited these locations  whereas Silent Hill being more grounded  in reality it's dungeons are shaped to  resemble locations akin to real-life  schools hospitals theme parks etc I have  no issue calling these locations  dungeons even  though the games nor the fanbase ever  refers to them as such  all the characteristics are there see if  this sounds familiar they are enclosed  spaces separate from the overworld you  can find a map to make your life a lot  easier  there's lock doors keys to collect  puzzles to solve enemies to fight and at  the end of each one there's usually a  boss fight waiting for you these so  called dungeons in Silent Hill are  perhaps less creative in terms of level  design than the ones we encounter in the  Zelda series since they are more  restricted to reality this is however  more fitting for a game of this genre as  a matter of fact one of the weaknesses  of some of the later games is that as  soon as they start to go too crazy with  some of the level designs it starts to  feel out of place  my favorite dungeon area in any of the  early Silent Hill games are the  hospitals one because well hospitals  already creeped me out in real life  amplify that with a decrepit and  abandoned hospital where you can barely  see your hand two inches from your face  because of your dinky little flashlight  and man the result is intoxicating  getting the map to any of these areas is  pretty much crucial unless you already  know the game on the back of your hand  there are loads and loads of doors to  try and different floors to visit it  gets confusing real quick as to which  doors you've already tried and which  rooms you've already checked out on top  of that backtracking to different floors  and rooms you've already visited is  frequently required but with the map at  your disposal things start to get a lot  more convenient and it creates oversight  the protagonist automatically makes  notes of which doors are inaccessible  which ones are locked and other things  of interest I think I would have  preferred to make my own notes but you  know drawing on a map with an analog  stick or god forbid a d-pad isn't really  an option because of the advantage of  the Wii remotes drawing on your map was  actually implemented in Silent Hill  shattered Memories but ironically the  game was so easy this was rarely ever  necessary there is one additional aspect  to the dungeon designs but I will save  that for a later part of the video as it  deserves its own category  [Music]  I've already given you a general  overview of how Silent Hill sets up a  scary atmosphere but what good would  atmosphere be if there's no actual  threats and scares to break the tension  this is of course where enemies come  into play one unique feature that has  been present from the very first game is  the radio  except for Silent Hill the room see what  I mean with this game anyway the radio  is usually obtained very early in the  game and serves as an indicator when  enemies are close as soon as they start  to come in proximity the radio will  start emitting static now you would  think that this ruins the suspense but  actually not that much even if you know  that an enemy is somewhere close you  still won't be able to see it until it's  already in your field of view  the static gets louder the closer the  enemy is and if you still feel like the  radio is ruining your suspense don't  worry you always have the option to turn  it off Silent Hill in general doesn't  feature the greatest variety of enemies  Silent Hill 2 for instance which is  regarded as one of the best in the  series actually only features six  regular enemies not counting bosses but  what they lack in quantity they make up  for in originality as opposed to your  run-of-the-mill typical zombie these  monsters all feature very unique designs  and behaviors monster designer masahito  ito strikes a great balance between  humanoid designs and features that make  it unrecognizable as a human making them  very unnerving the enemy AI is nothing  special but combat isn't exactly the  main focus of the series so much so that  I'm not even devoting a separate  category to it the controls are quite  clunky but this was a conscious decision  by the developers you're not meant to be  a hero or a soldier that just plows its  way through hordes of enemies in fact  it's usually the best option to just  avoid regular enemies as much as  possible and save your ammo for the boss  fights the enemies are mostly there to  contrast the desolate and lonely  atmosphere in between the boss fights  themselves are hit or miss due to these  simplistic and clunky controls it  usually doesn't come down to more than  just run around try not to get hit all  the while you study their attack pattern  to land a few hits in between their  moments of cooldown or vulnerability  aesthetically of course they are once  again very creepy and very unnerving and  in most cases masterfully designed even  if you've never played a single Silent  Hill game there is a good chance that  you will at least recognize this guy  mechanically the bosses are nothing  special but again it's not that type of  game where you have epic boss fights  with crazy stunts involved next I want  to go over the puzzles these are unlike  any of the ones you will find in any  Zelda game no block pushing here folks  these are genuine head scratchers and  some of them are notoriously hard to  figure out it also didn't help that team  silent didn't shy away from putting some  of the most difficult ones in the early  game this piano one right here me and my  cousins must have racked our brains for  several hours before we were finally  able to figure it out  what it comes down to is it's a poem  that describes the tale of birds without  a voice by interpreting the poem  correctly you will be able to figure out  which keys to press in what order this  is easier said than done however as the  poem itself is quite cryptic I'm not  going to give away the solution but this  is one of the earliest puzzles in the  game and I can imagine that a lot of  people got stuck at this point starting  and unfortunately also ending with  Silent Hill 2 &amp; 3  the development team made the games a  little bit more accessible by including  a separate riddle difficulty the higher  the difficulty the less clues and  contexts are given to the player this  way the games can be catered towards  different types of people some might not  enjoy head scratching and cryptic  puzzles that get you stuck for hours  upon end but they do want to challenge  in terms of combat and survival in which  case they can set the action difficulty  to hard and the riddle difficulty to  easy or of course vice versa  unfortunately every main line game after  Silent Hill 3 either had a general  difficulty setting or none at all  [Music]  we are already in the final stretch of  the video and not once have I ever  mentioned the other world or nightmare  world if you will this is one of the  most famous features in the entire  series and define Silent Hill as a town  with a mind of its own but I wanted to  save this one for the end as it impacts  everything we've covered previously in  stark contrast with the fog world as  it's commonly referred to when it's only  dark inside of the buildings in the  other world everything is dark even the  overworld the aesthetics become that of  rust blood and iron the atmosphere  shifts from desolate to hellish  industrial madness enemies are stronger  and more plentiful and this is usually  also where the boss fight takes place in  Silent Hill 2 the Otherworld is a little  less extreme and a bit gloomy er with  somewhat darker cooler colors with more  blues and greens involved the transition  to the Otherworld generally signifies  the halfway point of the dungeon what  this parallel dimension does is  essentially double the size of each  dungeon and yes most of these areas have  a fog world and an Otherworld equivalent  the layout of the area generally stays  the same with some minor differences  however doors that were previously  locked or jammed can now sometimes be  accessible and vice versa in some ways  you could compare this to the dark world  in a Link to the Past a dark reflection  of the light world however the big  difference being that in a Link to the  Past you have control over it in the  light world you are restricted to  portals but in the dark world you have  the option to travel back with the magic  mirror at any time Silent Hill origins  the first game to be developed outside  of Japan experimented with a similar  technique that gives the player the  option to transition to the other world  at will by the use of mirrors in this  case the mirrors are stationary and not  something you can carry with you in the  games from the team silent era you have  no control over which dimension you  visit once you transition to the other  world it takes a while before you return  to the fog world this is not a decision  that is up to you I personally like this  approach to the Otherworld the most as  it creates an oppressive feeling of  something that is out of your control an  external factor or presence that pulls  the strings which is regarded to be the  town of Silent Hill itself when the  choice to transition between dimensions  handed to the player it doesn't have the  same effect anymore it can present some  new puzzle opportunities yes but in my  opinion it's not worth sacrificing that  which makes the game so great in the  first place it's unique and foreboding  atmosphere puzzles based on dimension  shifting and stuff like that is better  reserved for other types of games if  you've never played a Silent Hill game  perhaps you would consider picking one  up sometime if of course you can deal  with the themes of horror if you're  afraid of jump scares don't worry there  are some mild jump scares but they are  very scarce and well spaced out Silent  Hill is all about psychological horror  and doesn't bombard you with obnoxiously  loud sounds and imagery anyway I'm still  hoping for the day that Silent Hill will  return to its glory days we got so close  with PT which was supposed to be a tease  for a brand new Japanese title but alas  it wasn't meant to be but there's always  hope I want to personally thank scribe  for giving me permission to use his  footage from his Let's Plays I know that  some people prefer to watch someone else  play a horror game rather than playing  it themselves in that case I highly  recommend watching his videos he has  playlists of him playing through Silent  Hill 1 2 3 and even some of the western  games he also doesn't just run through  but rather takes his time to explain  some of the lore the development cycle  symbolism and anything else that makes  the game so interesting for next week  I'm looking to see if I can give a shot  at some lore in Zelda yeah that will go  well I also have some other scripts and  ideas floating around so I will make  sure to get on those as quickly as  possible as always if you like my  content Duda that's the thing with the  button and the Bell stuff like that you  know what I'm talking about this is Don  signing off and have a good one  you

